        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Hallstatt culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in two zones divided at about 14–15° east: a western zone from north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Swiss Plateau through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and an eastern zone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>; key sites Hallstatt, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heuneburg">Heuneburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-lassois">Mont Lassois</a> and Vix, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>, the Burgstallkogel</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hallstatt A (1200–1050 BCE) and B (1050–800 BCE), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> Urnfield culture; Hallstatt C (800–620 BCE) and D (620–450 BCE), the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and the Hallstatt period proper, with D divided into D1–D3; followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the salt mines and great cemetery of Hallstatt; early iron swords; princely seats such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heuneburg">Heuneburg</a>; rich wagon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> burials under mounds, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a> and Vix; Greek and Etruscan imports such as the Vix krater; geometric metalwork and situla art; the Strettweg Cult Wagon</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavation of the Hallstatt cemetery by Johann Georg Ramsauer from 1846; Otto Tischler’s brooch studies (by 1881) and Paul Reinecke’s chronology (1902); dendrochronology, since radiocarbon dates are blurred by the “Hallstatt plateau”; a few inscriptions; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>; ancient DNA (Damgaard et al. 2018, Fischer et al. 2022, Gretzinger et al. 2024)</span></div>
