    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Hallstatt culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 3,000 years ago, the Hallstatt culture grew up in Europe, around the Alps.</p><p>It is named after Hallstatt, a lakeside village in Austria with a salt mine.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Its people worked bronze, and about 2,800 years ago they began making iron too.</p><p>Most were farmers in small villages, but at Hallstatt, digging salt made people rich.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silver.jpg" alt="Silver" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silver <span class="kid-tale-cr">Alchemist-hp, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</span></figcaption></figure><p>Chiefs lived in forts on hilltops, where smiths worked bronze, silver and gold.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heuneburg.jpg" alt="Heuneburg" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Heuneburg <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ulf at German Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>From Greeks and Etruscans in the south came amber, ivory, fine pots and probably wine.</p><p>A Celtic city named by the Greek writer Herodotus may be one of their forts, the Heuneburg.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave.jpg" alt="Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave <span class="kid-tale-cr">marsupium photography, CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Great chiefs were buried under big earth mounds, with wagons and rich goods.</p><p>A grave at Hochdorf held a bronze couch carried by little figures on single wheels.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture.jpg" alt="La Tène culture" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>La Tène culture <span class="kid-tale-cr">Dbachmann, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 2,400 years ago, their centres were destroyed or left, and the La Tène culture followed.</p><p>Salt has kept their cloth, wood and leather, and their treasures are now in museums.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Hallstatt culture grew out of the Urnfield culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> phases, from about 800 to 450 BCE, form the Hallstatt period proper.</li><li>The salt mines of Hallstatt, worked most intensively from the 8th to the 5th centuries BCE, made its community unusually rich, and the salt has preserved cloth, wood, leather and tools.</li><li>Late in the period fortified princely seats such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heuneburg">Heuneburg</a> traded with the Greek and Etruscan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, but at the end many centres were destroyed or abandoned and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> took their place.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Vix krater, a Greek vessel buried in the rich grave at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-lassois">Mont Lassois</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, is the largest known metal vessel from Western classical antiquity.</p></aside>
    </section>

